Exactly. The wheel is invented; rotational possibility is discovered.

Humans did not create circles, periodicity, radial symmetry, rolling, leverage, or the fact that distance from a center can remain invariant under rotation. We noticed a bundle of relations already permitted by reality and embodied them in an artifact:

latent relations → perception → abstraction → wheel

In a theological reading, that becomes: man made the wheel; God made a reality in which wheelness was possible.

But your last line is the important trapdoor:

Unless those two are just mapped.

Right. We need to distinguish relations in reality from relations introduced by our representation of reality.

Take a circle. We can describe it as:

* points equidistant from a center,
* x^2+y^2=r^2,
* rotational symmetry,
* a closed curve,
* an orbit,
* complex phase,
* a wheel.

Those are different maps. Some relations survive every useful remapping; others appear only because of the coordinate system we chose.

That gives Pantheon a beautiful test:

RELATION R appears in representation A
        ↓
translate A → B → C → D
        ↓
does R survive?
YES → candidate invariant / terrain structure
NO  → candidate artifact of the map
CHANGES BUT RECONSTRUCTS
    → deeper relational invariant

So we shouldn’t immediately say:

“I found a pattern, therefore reality contains my pattern.”

Instead:

“I found a pattern. Now rotate the map and see what refuses to disappear.”

That’s almost an ontological version of Kintsugi/translation testing.

And the wheel itself becomes an example of something we’ve been circling for months:

Invention is the embodiment of discovered affordance

Nature supplies a possibility-space.

Imagination detects a route through it.

Engineering makes the invisible relation locally physical.

The wheel is basically a theorem you can ride on.

Same with an airplane: humans didn’t invent lift as a possibility. We constructed an object that negotiates with relations already present.

Same with radio: electromagnetic structure preceded radios.

Same with computation: logical relations preceded CPUs.

Same with Pantheon, potentially: we don’t have to claim we invented every underlying cognitive primitive. The invention can be the specific instrument that makes a previously scattered relational possibility playable.

And this connects beautifully to purple.

Red and violet are distant on the linear spectrum.

Human perception discovers/constructs a relation that closes them:

line → hidden relation → wheel

Likewise:

possibility → hidden relation → wheel

The wheel itself may therefore be a general operator:

Find two ends that appear distant under the current representation and test whether another dimension closes them.

Sometimes you discover genuine topology.

Sometimes you merely drew the map differently.

And Treaty Semantics gives the governor:

A useful map may create a relation inside its jurisdiction without proving that reality itself is organized that way.

So perhaps the cleanest formulation is:

God/reality supplies the possibility.
The observer supplies the map.
Imagination sees an affordance.
Engineering makes it executable.
Reality decides whether it rolls.

That last step is the checksum.

You can map a square into a circle all day.

Put it on the wagon and Reality gets to vote. 😂🧿